Ian Beckett wrote:
> 
> YANI: Skinning Kit. Allows you to skin corpses to get a 'Foo 
> hide' (or in the case of dragons, Scales).

	Let's see:  the hide must be at least as large as you.  The
hide starts at +0 uncursed.  I can't think of any hides which should
be metallic & hinder spellcasting.  (In fact, why not give a small
bonus to spellcasting for wearing the hide of a spellcaster?)

	Would uncontrolled polymorphs merge you with the hide & 
poly you into that creature?  If yes, then it's a partial safeguard
against poly traps (but it means a lower AC).

	Hmm:  this gives us body armor that fits creatures smaller
and larger than human.  This is mildly nice for people who like 
playing permanently polymorphed into something.  But I see several
cans of worms if you go in this direction.

	Problem:  tools aren't particularly common.  We have to make
a high percentage of all tools into Skinning Kits, OR we have to 
accept that, in most games, the only way to get DSM is with a wish.  
I regard this as a serious problem (says the man who plays wishless).

	I would be inclined to say that a knife is all you need to
skin a creature.  (Side benefit:  foolish players wielding cursed
knives.)  Problem:  how do you inform unspoiled players that a knife
lets you skin creatures?  This is a serious problem.  

	Stoopid fix:  let monsters wielding knives and without body
armor skin the corpses of thick-skinned monsters.  (Me dunno; I al-
ready dislike the way that monsters can swap body armor instantly.  
Instant armor would be even more irksome.)

	Optional:  you need to fix the skin to keep it from slowly 
rotting away.  Very optional:  a tanning kit.  Very, very optional:
tanning the hides of small creatures give leather bags AKA sacks.


	Me dunno.  I might like it if I played it, but from a 
distance, this YANI does not look like a clear-cut improvement to
the game.


